<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinando I de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1549">1549</a>–<a href="/years/1609">1609</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinando I de' Medici was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> from 1587 to 1609. A cardinal who gave up his office to succeed his brother Francesco I, he governed ably, developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> as a free port open to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and other foreign merchants through the Livornine laws of 1591–1593, drained marshes and reduced Tuscany's dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-of-lorraine">Christina of Lorraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici.jpg" alt="Ferdinando I de' Medici" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scipione Pulzone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulzone,_Scipione_-_Ferdinando_I_de%27_Medici,_granduca_di_Toscana_-_1590.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinando was the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, and Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, daughter of the Spanish viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. He was made a cardinal in 1562 at the age of 13 but was never ordained a priest. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he proved an able administrator, founded the Villa Medici and acquired the antiquities collected by Andrea della Valle in 1584, along with other works such as the Medici lions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ferdinando succeeded his brother Francesco I in 1587, aged 38, and was in many ways his opposite: approachable and generous, he set out to rule mildly. He re-established the justice system, and Tuscany regained the independence his brother had given up. He founded the Medici Oriental Press, which published many books in Arabic script, and he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> a refuge for Spanish Jews and other persecuted groups. He expanded the harbour built by Cosimo I, rerouted part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> into the Naviglio canal to link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, and promoted an irrigation project in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana">Val di Chiana</a>.</p><p>He kept his cardinal's position for two years, then gave it up to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-of-lorraine">Christina of Lorraine</a> in 1589; her dowry included 600,000 crowns in cash. His foreign policy sought to free Tuscany from Spanish domination. After the assassination of Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1589 he financed Henry IV, urged him to embrace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and used his influence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> to win acknowledgement of the conversion. He also obtained formal investiture of Siena, strengthened the Tuscan fleet, which defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates in 1607 and a larger Turkish fleet the next year, and in 1608 sent an expedition under Captain Robert Thornton towards northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1600 his court staged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacopo-peri">Jacopo Peri</a>'s Euridice to mark the marriage of his niece Marie de' Medici to Henry IV, an early landmark in the emergence of opera. Ferdinando died on 17 February 1609 and was succeeded as grand duke by his son Cosimo II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinando I de' Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinando I</b>’s laws of 1591 and 1593, known as the Livornine, invited merchants of all nations and religions to settle there, granting Jews freedom of worship, protection and trading rights; Livorno became a cosmopolitan free port with one of the largest Jewish communities in Italy and a hub…</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1588" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ferdinando I de' Medici"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1588">What was here in 1588? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
